 you agree with that chris if  you can uh sort of perhaps look into  putin's mind simply because you're there  in moscow  do you think that this is his outlook  yeah look i i think first of all as we  said russia  views these relationships over the very  long term and it is actually important  to note that  the the  russian government uh always made a  point that it will always honor  contracts so in this situation it's  actually make it's about making like  life difficult for the european  customers rather than perhaps risking  the contracts because  you know german or other european  customers can still pay in dollars or  euros as per the contract but they now  have to pay into gazprom bank  rather than to gazprom and hence the  conversion to rubles from there so  essentially what russia is saying is  that technically the contract is not  disrupted it is only the payment  mechanism that has changed and therefore  they're again throwing the onus on the  on europe and the european customers to  uh as well as eu commission president  said to break sanctions but but russia  itself is saying we're actually not  doing much to the contracts only  changing where you pay not how you pay  are the terms of the contract itself so  in that sense of course russia is in a  strong position  although you really have to  always reflect on the fact that  russia and europe are in a mutually  difficult position europe needs to guess  but russia needs the money  so in that sense you're not going to get  a situation where russia will just  arbitrarily turn the gas off because it  needs the financial lifeline in order to  sustain the economy and provide  employment subsidies social subsidies  etc uh the situation in russia will be  very difficult  difficult and very different tomorrow if  those gas flows were cut off and the  money stream was disrupted so in that  sense there's a lot of bluffing there's  a game of brinksmanship there's a game  of chicken it'll play out over the  summer w