 all  of that is massively accelerated at this  point  so chris if this does backfire  does russia risk becoming an economic  prior as the uk has said it will do and  does it risk putin's very  future well i think when you say  backfire i i think it is now absolutely  inevitable that europe will buy less  russian energy regardless of what  happens next but regardless of  you know what happens to sanctions etc i  think that's now that game is now to  change uh for good uh but in russia is  it's also a case that russia does not  want to be as dependent on europe as a  its major energy customer uh either and  for example since 2014 uh when when  europe started talking about russia  russian energy  risk in moscow the conversation was  about also russia's exposure to european  europe as an energy customer and since  2014 we've seen major projects being  built uh focusing on china in gas and  oil and lng and and the discussion in  moscow right now is acknowledging that  europe has begun to become steadily and  less important  customer but asia china india particular  are all going to become bigger customers  and compensate so the view in moscow is  that this is about rebalancing it is  inevitable that europe will go down as a  customer but they will be compensated  with new customer growth elsewhere  that's the way they're looking at it  right now uh so therefore the view in  moscow is that it's rebalancing and it's  not going to destroy the economy but  clearly it all depends on on scale and  time and the urgency that this uh that  this will all be uh rolled out over um  what are your thoughts on that  yeah no i know that's a really really  interesting point this is something we  work a lot with with our clients as well  trying to understand the re-routing  capabilities  for uh for  natural gas and what we have seen and  just speaking of how it looks like  looking right now is that we see that we  have around  the export capacity going going  going east into china that's around uh  at max 400 hours per year if you compare  that what's going flowing into the  western european market through the  yamal pipeline the northern one pipeline  and also the major  brotherhood pipeline going through  ukraine that's around 1700. so then you  get the idea of the scale of the  european market and that's a really  really interesting  interesting topic that i i totally agree  russians are really really reliant  enough  at us as well but we also see that there  is um expansion plans for the for  example the power of siberia a huge part  of angola in china which is but that's  scheduled to come online uh at around  20 20 30. it's you know it's  more than one eight years so so um  i think it's important to have have that  and have that in mind  just ask them now europe is just too  large customer  yeah all plans do seem to be hinging on  2030. it's going to be interesting to  see how many of them can be and will be  sped up to deal with this particular  crisis andreas if there was ever a time  to invest in renewable energy it's now  or rather it was before now with the  climate crisis ever present and now  these supplies of fossil fuels uncertain  are we seeing enough effort down that  road  well i mean  i think it's the best time ever to do  that particularly in europe and in  places where government support is is  being ramped up and where you not only  have an economic case but also a strong  security case now a national security  case  as some politicians would make  would make it but um uh i think the the  one bottleneck here obviously is global  supply chains and what we have seen is  is some of the material uh and and some  of the parts that are needed  to really ramp up uh a renewable  capacity are not available because of  various bottlenecks across the globe and  because of um you know the knock-on  effects of a covert crisis still so you  know  on the one side we have aspiration and  good business cases and and i guess  great opportunity 